  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY NIGHT THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 309 AM CDT Thu Oct 8 2026\n\nThe presence of a strong low level jet Friday night, east of a \nsurface trough of low pressure over the Nebraska Panhandle, will\nlead to a well mixed boundary layer Friday night into Saturday \nmorning. With a mixed boundary layer, mild temperatures will \ncontinue into the overnight hours and Saturday morning. The \nlatest NBM ensemble forecast has a low of 62 for LBF Saturday \nmorning. This is around 10 degrees warmer than the latest MET \nand MAV lows for Saturday morning. I did go and trend lows down \nsomewhat from the inherited NBM forecast but not as low as the \nMET and MAV numbers would suggest. This led to a low around 60 \nfor North Platte Saturday morning which would be a record high \nminimum temperature for October 10th (record is 59). Gusty winds\nwill transition into central and eastern portions of the \nforecast area on Saturday as the surface trough migrates into \nthe eastern Panhandle and western Sandhills. Very warm \ntemperatures are expected Saturday afternoon with highs ranging \nfrom the middle 80s to around 90 degrees. Highs were raised some\nfrom the initialized NBM forecast to bring them more in line \nwith the warmer MET and MAV guidance. This yielded highs around \n90 over SW Nebraska and in the Valentine area. Record highs for \nSaturday are well into the mid 90s for North Platte, Valentine, \nand Imperial so record highs are not anticipated attm. Minimum \nRH Saturday afternoon will approach 15 to 20 percent in the \neastern Panhandle and NW Sandhills. However, with these areas \nINVOF the surface trough, winds are expected to remain below \ncritical criteria of 25 MPH. Stronger winds are expected over \neastern portions of the forecast area, however, minimum RH in \nthese areas will range from 30 to 40 percent. Will mention the \nelevated to near critical fire weather concerns in the HWO and \nhit it hard in the fire weather forecast. A cold front will \nbegin to push into far northwestern Nebraska by Sunday morning. \nWith the forecast area being well ahead of the front Saturday \nnight, very mild lows are expected. Lows will range from around \n50 in the northwest to the lower 60s in the east, where winds \nare expected to persist overnight. For North Platte, the minimum\nof 60 degrees would still be below the record high minimum of \n63 degrees for October 11. The cold front will stall across \nnorthwestern Nebraska on Sunday. This will lead to another mild \nday for most of the forecast area with highs into the 70s and \nlower 80s. Highs will be in the middle to upper 60s over \nnorthwestern portions of the forecast area INVOF the front. High\npressure will track into the western Dakotas on Monday, forcing\nthe front south into Kansas. The upper air pattern will amplify\nacross the western CONUS Monday night into Tuesday, leading to \nsouthwesterly flow aloft across the high plains. A lead \ndisturbance will ride over the stalled out surface front, \ninitiating precipitation over eastern Colorado and western \nKansas. Some of this activity may lift into the forecast area \nMonday night. Beyond Monday night, mainly dry conditions are \nexpected Tuesday with a decent threat for precipitation Tuesday \nnight into Wednesday-per the latest NBM forecast. This seems to \nagree with the latest deterministic GFS soln, however there are \nsome notable differences between the latest EC soln with respect\nto precipitation chances or lack there of on Tuesday. For now, \na dry forecast will be retained on Tuesday, however, there may \nbe a need to introduce precipitation chances with subsequent \nforecasts.",
